Before 2017, banks wrote **internal rules** to match prescribed steps. The 2017 amendment replaced that with a **risk-based approach**: the bank decides how much due diligence a client or product needs, based on its own assessment of risk, and records the reasoning.

Section 42 says the RMCP must:
- **identify, assess, monitor, mitigate and manage** the risk that the bank's new and existing products or services are used for money laundering, terrorist financing or proliferation financing;
- set out *how* the bank does CDD, beneficial ownership, ongoing due diligence, PEP checks, EDD and simplified due diligence, record keeping and reporting;
- explain any paragraph of s 42(2) that does not apply, and **why** (s 42(2A));
- be **approved** by the board or senior management (s 42(2B)) and **reviewed** at regular intervals (s 42(2C));
- be **available to every employee** involved in transactions covered by the Act (s 42(3)).

The PA does not approve a bank's RMCP. The bank owns it, and must show it works.

Also on **20 June 2025**, the PA issued a caution and a reprimand to the **Bank of Taiwan, South Africa Branch** for s 42 failures found in a 2022 inspection. The branch could not provide evidence that:
- material amendments to its RMCP manual were **approved** by its executive committee before being implemented;
- the **yearly review** of the manual, required by its own governance policy, was properly recorded;
- its screening manual had been **reviewed and approved** after 2020.

It also had not implemented its RMCP due diligence on two **correspondent banking (vostro)** relationships.

**Lesson:** approval and review are not paperwork for its own sake. Sections 42(2B) and 42(2C) make them legal duties, and the PA checks the evidence.

On **20 December 2024** the SARB announced sanctions on **Capitec Bank Limited** after FIC Act inspections in 2021 (retail) and 2022 (business banking): seven cautions, one reprimand and penalties totalling **R56.25 million**, of which R10.5 million was conditionally suspended for 36 months.

The largest finding concerned **CDD, EDD and ongoing due diligence** under ss 21 and 21A-21H, with gaps including:
- verifying client identity and **beneficial owners** of legal entities;
- obtaining or verifying **address and source of funds**;
- **PEP screening** and annual reviews of high-risk clients;
- **senior management approval** when re-rating or reviewing high-risk clients.

Those CDD findings alone carried R35 million in penalties across the two segments (R7 million suspended).

**Lesson:** CDD failures in sampled files add up, even at a large and well-resourced bank.

On **24 January 2025** the PA announced sanctions on **The Standard Bank of South Africa Limited** after a 2022 inspection: six cautions and penalties totalling **R13 million**. The findings included:
- **17,259** STRs/SARs reported late (s 29): R4 million penalty;
- **one** STR not reported at all (s 29): R1 million;
- **1,466** cash threshold or aggregation reports not filed on time (s 28): caution;
- under FIC Directive 5 of 2019, **75,729** monitoring alerts not attended to within 48 hours and **94,558** alerts closed after the 15-day reporting period in regulation 24(3): R8 million;
- no ongoing due diligence reviews for two clients in 2018 and 2019, and missing records of when 43 reports were submitted.

**Lesson:** at scale, slow alert handling becomes a reporting breach. Speed is part of compliance.

Section 26B applies to **every person**, not just banks. Nobody may, directly or indirectly, in whole or in part:
- acquire, collect, use, possess or own property, or **provide property, financial or other services or economic support**, intending or knowing (or when they ought reasonably to have known or suspected) that it will benefit, be used on behalf of, or be controlled by a designated person or entity;
- **deal with** or facilitate any transaction involving property of a designated person, or of someone **acting on their behalf or at their direction** (added in 2022);
- join an arrangement that helps a designated person **keep or control** the property, **convert** it, **conceal** its nature or ownership, move it **out of a jurisdiction** or put it in a **nominee's** name.

The Minister may permit some dealings under **s 26C**, such as basic expenses (food, rent, medicine, taxes, utilities), pre-existing judgments or contracts, and interest accruing on frozen accounts. A bank never decides that on its own.
